# ── granite.build environment configuration ───────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values you need.
# The analytics service (gb_ui_backend) reads GB_UI_* vars from this file automatically.
#
# Minimum for standalone mode (dashboard only, no analytics):
#   GBSERVER_AUTH_MODE, GBSERVER_METADATA_STORAGE
#
# Add GB_UI_GBSERVER_DB_URL to enable the Build volume chart and Leaderboard.
# Add GB_UI_DATABASE_URL for full analytics and AI analysis (SQLite or PostgreSQL).

# ── gbserver ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Authentication mode: apikey (standalone/local), github, ibmid, or multi
GBSERVER_AUTH_MODE=apikey

# Storage backend: sqlite (local dev) or sql (PostgreSQL for production)
GBSERVER_METADATA_STORAGE=sqlite

# Optional: set a shared secret for API access. If unset, localhost is allowed
# without auth in apikey mode (convenient for local dev).
# GBSERVER_API_KEY=

# Optional: your display name in the dashboard greeting
# GBSERVER_API_USER=alice

# ── Analytics service (gb_ui_backend) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Set GB_UI_GBSERVER_DB_URL to enable the Build volume chart and Leaderboard
# without needing PostgreSQL. If gb_ui_backend is installed, gbserver includes
# its routers directly into its own process at startup when either DB
# variable below is set.

# Path to gbserver's own SQLite database (enables Build volume + Leaderboard).
# The default location after running `gbserver standalone` at least once:
# GB_UI_GBSERVER_DB_URL=sqlite+aiosqlite:////Users/you/.granite.build/llmb-server.db

# The analytics service's own database (enables full analytics, failure
# trends, AI). SQLite (local, no setup needed) or PostgreSQL (shared/production):
# GB_UI_DATABASE_URL=sqlite+aiosqlite:///${HOME}/.granite.build/dashboard-analytics.db
# GB_UI_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://user:password@localhost:5432/gbui

# Used for the standalone dev-mode startup banner when running gb_ui_backend
# outside gbserver (python -m gb_ui_backend); not needed in the normal case.
GB_UI_GBSERVER_URL=http://localhost:8080

# ── Model Customization / AutoTuneX (optional) ────────────────────────────────
# Where the AutoTuneX server listens. gbserver reverse-proxies the dashboard's
# /api/autotunex/* calls to this URL's /api/v1/* (see api/autotunex_proxy.py), so
# the browser stays same-origin and needs no CORS. Leave the dashboard's Model
# Customization pages alone and they simply show an unreachable-backend error.
#
# WARNING: /api/autotunex/* is exempt from gbserver auth for every method, and
# AutoTuneX itself defaults to auth_providers=["disabled"] (no auth). Note this
# is NOT conditional on the variable below: root_api.py mounts the proxy route
# unconditionally and AUTOTUNEX_URL falls back to http://localhost:8000, so every
# deployment carries an unauthenticated relay to whatever listens there. Anyone
# who can reach gbserver's port can POST /api/autotunex/jobs (launching a real
# build) or DELETE datasets and configurations, even with GBSERVER_API_KEY or
# OIDC configured. Only run gbserver bound to localhost. See api/auth.py.
# AUTOTUNEX_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# ── AI analysis and chat assistant (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Both features call out to an LLM. AI analysis (failure-cause summaries, stored
# per build) also needs GB_UI_DATABASE_URL (PostgreSQL) to persist results —
# without it, analytics still work but AI analysis is disabled. The chat
# assistant (the floating widget in the dashboard) is enabled by either the
# GB_UI_* vars below or the Anthropic vars in the next section — see that
# section for the precedence rule if both are set.
#
# GB_UI_LLM_* configures the OpenAI-compatible endpoint both features use by
# default.
#
# Option A — Local Ollama (free, private, runs on your machine):
#   Install: https://ollama.com  then: ollama pull granite3.2
#   GB_UI_LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
#   GB_UI_LLM_API_KEY=ollama
#   GB_UI_LLM_MODELS=granite3.2
#
# Option B — IBM watsonx (cloud):
#   GB_UI_LLM_BASE_URL=https://us-south.ml.cloud.ibm.com/ml/v1/text/generation
#   GB_UI_LLM_API_KEY=<your-ibm-api-key>
#   GB_UI_LLM_MODELS=ibm/granite-3-3-8b-instruct
#
# Option C — Any OpenAI-compatible API:
#   GB_UI_LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#   GB_UI_LLM_API_KEY=sk-...
#   GB_UI_LLM_MODELS=gpt-4o-mini
#
# GB_UI_LLM_BASE_URL=
# GB_UI_LLM_API_KEY=
# GB_UI_LLM_MODELS=granite-4.0-h-small,granite-3.3-8b-instruct
#
# Chat assistant only, from here down. Requires the `chat` extra (bundled into
# `standalone`): pip install -e '.[chat]'
#
# Which ChatAgentBackend implementation to run — "tool_loop" is the only one
# that exists today:
# GB_UI_CHAT_BACKEND=tool_loop
#
# Which model provider tool_loop should use — "openai_compatible" or
# "anthropic". Leave unset to auto-detect: the OpenAI-compatible config below
# wins if configured (the natural default for a self-hosted deployment — no
# external API key, no request data leaving it), falling back to Anthropic
# only if that's all that's configured. Set this explicitly if you have both
# configured and want a specific one, or just want the choice to be stated
# rather than inferred:
# GB_UI_CHAT_PROVIDER=openai_compatible
#
# Model identifier for whichever provider ends up active: an Anthropic model ID
# if GB_UI_CHAT_PROVIDER=anthropic (or it's auto-detected), otherwise an
# LLMClient-style spec (a plain Ollama tag, or RITS's "slug:full/name" form)
# for GB_UI_CHAT_LLM_BASE_URL below, or GB_UI_LLM_BASE_URL above if that's
# unset too. Leave unset to fall back to a default for whichever provider
# ends up active (claude-sonnet-5 for Anthropic; GB_UI_LLM_MODELS' first
# entry otherwise):
# GB_UI_CHAT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
#
# Override GB_UI_LLM_BASE_URL/GB_UI_LLM_API_KEY above — only set these if the
# chat assistant should use a different OpenAI-compatible model/endpoint than
# AI analysis. Most setups don't need these at all:
# GB_UI_CHAT_LLM_BASE_URL=
# GB_UI_CHAT_LLM_API_KEY=

# ── Anthropic overrides (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lets the chat assistant use Claude directly instead of the OpenAI-compatible
# config above. Auto-detected if GB_UI_CHAT_PROVIDER is unset and no
# OpenAI-compatible config is present above; set GB_UI_CHAT_PROVIDER=anthropic
# to use this even when both are configured. Requires the separate
# `chat-anthropic` extra — NOT bundled into the base `chat`/`standalone`
# extras, since Anthropic is opt-in, not the default:
# pip install -e '.[chat-anthropic]'
#
# These are NOT GB_UI_-prefixed — they're the standard Anthropic SDK
# credentials, read directly from the environment by the `anthropic` package
# itself. Two mutually exclusive forms — set ONE, not both (the client sends
# both headers and the API rejects the request if both are set):
#
# Option A — Direct Anthropic API. Get a key from console.anthropic.com (your
# workspace → Settings → API Keys). Billed pay-as-you-go.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# Option B — Route through an internal LLM gateway/proxy instead. Ask whoever
# manages that gateway for a credential meant for a separate long-running
# service — don't reuse a personal/session-scoped token.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ── Cloud logs (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Enables the Logs tab on running builds. Requires a cloud logging service
# instance (e.g. IBM Cloud Logs).
# GB_UI_CLOUD_LOGS_URL=
# GB_UI_CLOUD_LOGS_API_KEY=
